diff --git a/docs/bots.md b/docs/bots.md
index 9dacea2..51c637d 100644
--- a/docs/bots.md
+++ b/docs/bots.md
@@ -6,8 +6,13 @@ Bots are represented in the `users` table with `role: :bot` (`enum :role, %i[ me
 
 - **`creator_id`**: Foreign key to `users` (`belongs_to :creator, class_name: "User", optional: true`), recording which chat member created the bot. Because `User::Role#can_administer?` checks `administrator? || self == record&.creator`, creators and administrators automatically have permission to manage a bot while other regular members do not.
 - **`bot_token` & `bot_key`**: Active bots receive a unique 24-character `SecureRandom.base58` `bot_token` on creation. The externally visible bot code (`User#bot_key`) is `"#{id}-#{bot_token}"`. `User.authenticate_bot(bot_key)` looks up the active bot by primary key `id` and compares `bot_token` in constant time via `ActiveSupport::SecurityUtils.secure_compare`.
-- **Deactivation (Deletion)**: Deleting a bot calls `User#deactivate`, which sets `status: :deactivated`, clears `bot_token: nil`, and removes non-direct room memberships while preserving historical messages authored by the bot.
-- **Room Memberships**: Because bots are `User` records with `status: :active`, they automatically join all open rooms via `after_create_commit :grant_membership_to_open_rooms` (and `Rooms::Open#grant_access_to_all_users`) and appear alongside human users in closed/direct room member selectors (`User.active`). Human-only flows (`/account/edit` member list, `/session`, session transfers, IP bans) scope queries with `User.without_bots`.
+- **`auto_join_open_rooms`**: Boolean (`default: false, null: false`), controlled by the `"Add to all open rooms"` switch on `/bots/new` and `/bots/:id/edit`.
+- **Deactivation (Deletion)**: Deleting a bot calls `User#deactivate`, which sets `status: :deactivated`, clears `bot_token: nil`, destroys its webhook, and removes non-direct room memberships while preserving historical messages authored by the bot.
+- **Room Memberships**:
+  - Human users join all open rooms by default (`User#grant_membership_to_open_rooms` and `Rooms::Open#grant_access_to_all_users` via `User.open_room_grantees`).
+  - Bots do **not** join open rooms by default (`auto_join_open_rooms: false`). Room admins/creators can toggle individual bots on open rooms (`Rooms::OpensController` / `app/views/rooms/opens/`), closed rooms (`Rooms::ClosedsController`), and direct rooms (`Rooms::DirectsController`).
+  - When `auto_join_open_rooms` is switched on (`true`), the bot joins all existing open rooms (`after_save_commit :grant_membership_to_open_rooms`) and every open room created or converted from closed afterward (`User.open_room_grantees`). Turning `auto_join_open_rooms` off keeps the bot's current memberships.
+  - Human-only flows (`/account/edit` member list, `/session`, session transfers, IP bans) scope queries with `User.without_bots`.
 
 ## Bot HTTP API (`Messages::ByBotsController`)
 
@@ -20,9 +25,14 @@ Bots are represented in the `users` table with `role: :bot` (`enum :role, %i[ me
 
 - **Data Model**: `Webhook` (`webhooks` table with `user_id` foreign key and `url`) belongs to `User` (`has_one :webhook, dependent: :destroy, autosave: true` in `User::Bot`). `User::Bot` exposes `webhook_url` and `webhook_url=` so bot forms (`/bots/new` and `/bots/:id/edit`) can create, update, or clear a bot's webhook (saving an empty `webhook_url` destroys the `Webhook` record; `User#deactivate` also destroys it).
 - **URL Policy**: Validates `URI::HTTP` (`http`/`https`) with a present host, allowing self-hosted/internal/localhost endpoints without requiring external DNS resolution.
-- **@-Mention Autocomplete & Dispatch**: Active bots in a room appear in `Autocompletable::UsersController` alongside human members and use standard ActionText `User` attachables (`User::Mentionable`). On `Message` creation, `Room#receive` calls `deliver_webhooks_later`, which enqueues a `Webhook::DeliveryJob` (on the `default` queue) per active bot with a webhook in `message.mentionees` (ignoring bot-authored messages to prevent bot loops, and never blocking the HTTP request of the user posting the message).
-- **Payload & Stripped Command Text**: Posts `Content-Type: application/json` with `{ event: "mention", author, room: { id, name, reply_to }, message: { id, body: { text, html }, link } }`. `Message::Mentionee#plain_text_without_mention(bot)` strips only that bot's own `<action-text-attachment>` nodes from the rich-text fragment while preserving other mentions and `message.body.html`.
-- **Synchronous vs. Asynchronous Replies**:
+- **`joined` Event**:
+  - `Membership.grant_for` uses `insert_all(..., returning: %i[ room_id user_id ])` (and `Membership` also defines `after_create_commit :deliver_joined_webhook_later` for single-record `create`), ensuring `Webhook::DeliveryJob` is enqueued after transaction commit (`ActiveRecord.after_all_transactions_commit`) **strictly for newly inserted memberships** of active bots with webhooks in non-direct rooms (`Room.without_directs`).
+  - Direct conversations (`Rooms::Direct`) never trigger `joined`.
+  - Posts `Content-Type: application/json` with `{ event: "joined", room: { id, name, reply_to } }`.
+- **`mention` Event**:
+  - Active bots in a room appear in `Autocompletable::UsersController` alongside human members and use standard ActionText `User` attachables (`User::Mentionable`). On `Message` creation, `Room#receive` calls `deliver_webhooks_later`, which enqueues a `Webhook::DeliveryJob` per active bot with a webhook in `message.mentionees` (ignoring bot-authored messages to prevent bot loops, and never blocking the HTTP request of the user posting the message).
+  - Posts `Content-Type: application/json` with `{ event: "mention", author, room: { id, name, reply_to }, message: { id, body: { text, html }, link } }`. `Message::Mentionee#plain_text_without_mention(bot)` strips only that bot's own `<action-text-attachment>` nodes from the rich-text fragment while preserving other mentions and `message.body.html`.
+- **Synchronous vs. Asynchronous Replies (for both `joined` and `mention`)**:
   - `200` with `Content-Type: text/plain` escapes HTML entities and converts newlines to `<br>`, posting and broadcasting the message as the bot.
   - `200` with `Content-Type: text/html` posts and broadcasts rich-text HTML (sanitized by ActionText and `ContentFilters`) as the bot.
   - `204` (or non-text/non-200 responses) posts nothing synchronously; the bot can post asynchronously at any time via `room.reply_to` (`/rooms/:room_id/:bot_key/messages`).
